WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.343 --> 00:00:02.112
- And when I went to my boss and I said,

00:00:02.112 --> 00:00:03.804
look, I'd like to design these ties

00:00:03.804 --> 00:00:05.162
'cause I think they could be new,

00:00:05.162 --> 00:00:08.220
he said the world isn't
ready for Ralph Lauren.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.864
I learned to trust my own sense.

00:00:10.864 --> 00:00:14.966
Everything I've always done has been for

00:00:14.966 --> 00:00:16.080
my own emotion.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.008
Movies and books open the world for you.

00:00:20.008 --> 00:00:22.534
There was no miracle, there
was no snap of the finger.

00:00:22.534 --> 00:00:24.605
People didn't just say
you're wonderful, in fact.

00:00:24.605 --> 00:00:27.854
The way I work is, I feel
like I'm doing movies.

00:00:27.854 --> 00:00:29.425
And all of a sudden, I got a phone call

00:00:29.425 --> 00:00:31.566
from a banker and said, Mr. Lauren,

00:00:31.566 --> 00:00:34.616
your bills are not being paid on time.

00:00:34.616 --> 00:00:35.846
I wanted to be a baseball player,

00:00:35.846 --> 00:00:37.080
I wanted to be a basketball player.

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.363
I wanted to be an actor,
I wanted to be a dancer.

00:00:39.363 --> 00:00:42.457
Cancer is not only for the
poor and it's not for the rich.

00:00:42.457 --> 00:00:44.771
It happens to everybody.

00:00:44.771 --> 00:00:46.500
He's an American fashion designer,

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.822
philanthropist and business executive.

00:00:48.822 --> 00:00:50.596
He's best known for founding a global

00:00:50.596 --> 00:00:52.913
mult-billion dollar clothing empire

00:00:52.913 --> 00:00:54.396
that bears his name.

00:00:54.396 --> 00:00:57.454
Forbes estimates his net
worth to be 8 billion dollars.

00:00:57.454 --> 00:00:58.734
He's Ralph Lauren,

00:00:58.734 --> 00:01:02.234
and here are his top 10 rules for success.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.751
- So this all started with a tie.

00:01:06.751 --> 00:01:09.357
A lot of people think it
started with the polo shirt,

00:01:09.357 --> 00:01:10.883
but it started with the tie.

00:01:10.883 --> 00:01:13.195
- It started way back.

00:01:13.195 --> 00:01:15.921
I felt I had something to say.

00:01:15.921 --> 00:01:17.897
There were no fashion designers
when I was growing up.

00:01:17.897 --> 00:01:21.021
The idea of fashion and what
fashion has become today

00:01:21.021 --> 00:01:23.365
was very different.

00:01:23.365 --> 00:01:24.436
I was working.

00:01:24.436 --> 00:01:25.465
I was working in a job

00:01:25.465 --> 00:01:28.548
and I wasn't sure who I was going to be.

00:01:29.448 --> 00:01:31.213
- What was the job?

00:01:31.213 --> 00:01:34.057
- I used to hang up returns
in a department store

00:01:34.057 --> 00:01:36.158
when I was 16 years old,

00:01:36.158 --> 00:01:38.934
when I was still going to high school.

00:01:38.934 --> 00:01:42.513
Along the way, I felt that
I had something to say

00:01:42.513 --> 00:01:44.096
that was different.

00:01:45.456 --> 00:01:47.205
No one believed me, of course.

00:01:47.205 --> 00:01:49.154
When I went to my boss and I said look,

00:01:49.154 --> 00:01:51.132
I'd like to design these
ties 'cause I think

00:01:51.132 --> 00:01:52.003
they could be new,

00:01:52.003 --> 00:01:55.760
he said the world isn't
ready for Ralph Lauren.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:56.593
- Wow.

00:01:56.593 --> 00:01:58.772
- I've never forgot that,
because even when he said that,

00:01:58.772 --> 00:02:00.383
the world isn't ready for you,

00:02:00.383 --> 00:02:01.578
I thought that was a compliment.

00:02:01.578 --> 00:02:02.411
- Yes.

00:02:02.411 --> 00:02:05.035
- So what happens, fellow
came along and said,

00:02:05.035 --> 00:02:06.566
Ralph, would you like to come with me?

00:02:06.566 --> 00:02:10.120
And I said I would, I'd like
to start my own division.

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.985
Called it Polo,

00:02:11.985 --> 00:02:15.235
and they gave me a drawer in
the Empire State Building.

00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:17.677
Not an office, but a drawer.

00:02:17.677 --> 00:02:18.510
- [Oprah] A drawer.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:19.698
- I worked on the bottom of the drawer,

00:02:19.698 --> 00:02:21.681
and everyone in the office thought,

00:02:21.681 --> 00:02:24.197
who is this little kid
working out of the drawer?

00:02:24.197 --> 00:02:27.762
And I took the samples,
made these wide ties,

00:02:27.762 --> 00:02:29.845
and delivered them myself

00:02:30.864 --> 00:02:32.891
in a bomber jacket and jeans,

00:02:32.891 --> 00:02:34.874
and that's how it went.

00:02:34.874 --> 00:02:37.727
When I went to Bloomingdale's,
the buyer said,

00:02:37.727 --> 00:02:41.000
we like your ties Ralph, but
would you make them narrower?

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:41.998
(laughing)

00:02:41.998 --> 00:02:45.171
And would you, we don't
want to use your label,

00:02:45.171 --> 00:02:46.906
we want to use our own label.

00:02:46.906 --> 00:02:49.140
But we'll give you a really good order.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.473
Now here I was, no business,

00:02:52.757 --> 00:02:54.089
and hungry,

00:02:54.089 --> 00:02:56.076
and I said to the buyer, I said,

00:02:56.076 --> 00:02:57.188
my heart's in my throat,

00:02:57.188 --> 00:02:59.688
or whatever the expression is,

00:03:01.092 --> 00:03:03.303
I would love to sell Bloomingdale's.

00:03:03.303 --> 00:03:04.576
They were the hottest store,

00:03:04.576 --> 00:03:07.100
young, hot store, I said but,

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:08.417
I can't sell you.

00:03:08.417 --> 00:03:10.893
I'm closing my bag and
I'm not going to sell you.

00:03:10.893 --> 00:03:12.669
Six months later they called me back.

00:03:12.669 --> 00:03:13.502
- [Interviewer] Wow.

00:03:13.502 --> 00:03:17.173
- And they said Ralph, we
can't find your ties anywhere

00:03:17.173 --> 00:03:18.656
and we want to buy them,

00:03:18.656 --> 00:03:20.138
and we want to put a rack in.

00:03:20.138 --> 00:03:23.138
Rack of ties amongst the other ties.

00:03:25.316 --> 00:03:26.903
That was it.

00:03:26.903 --> 00:03:29.415
The breakthrough was they bought it.

00:03:29.415 --> 00:03:31.555
And the breakthrough for
me is that I've learned

00:03:31.555 --> 00:03:33.373
to trust my instincts.

00:03:33.373 --> 00:03:36.130
I learned to trust my own sense.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:38.437
'Cause the normal thing to do is say,

00:03:38.437 --> 00:03:40.297
I'll make it any way you want to.

00:03:40.297 --> 00:03:42.443
That's how the world was,

00:03:42.443 --> 00:03:43.927
I want to sell you the ties,

00:03:43.927 --> 00:03:46.111
maybe I'll have a new
fashion for you next year,

00:03:46.111 --> 00:03:47.347
let me buy it.

00:03:47.347 --> 00:03:50.572
My philosophy was that I loved these ties,

00:03:50.572 --> 00:03:51.644
I made them.

00:03:51.644 --> 00:03:55.370
They were not just a business to me,

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:57.787
they were my voice to myself.

00:03:58.957 --> 00:04:02.308
Everything I've always done has been for

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:05.641
my own emotion and it was never done for

00:04:07.498 --> 00:04:09.498
a brand or a label, it's

00:04:11.105 --> 00:04:12.543
because I feel it.

00:04:12.543 --> 00:04:14.481
And when you give it a title,

00:04:14.481 --> 00:04:16.590
then it sort of takes it away for me.

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:17.840
When I grew up,

00:04:19.031 --> 00:04:21.132
in terms of at that age,

00:04:21.132 --> 00:04:23.198
I don't know if I had an identity,

00:04:23.198 --> 00:04:26.781
but I remember years
ago, I don't know why,

00:04:28.326 --> 00:04:32.023
there was a pair of
suede shoes in the store,

00:04:32.023 --> 00:04:34.130
blue suede shoes.

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:35.736
And it was before Elvis Presley

00:04:35.736 --> 00:04:38.819
had the song called Blue Suede Shoes.

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.760
On my way to school,
I would pass the store

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:45.927
that had these suede shoes and I said,

00:04:46.816 --> 00:04:49.465
I got to have those shoes,
how am I going to get them?

00:04:49.465 --> 00:04:53.238
And every day after school
I passed these shoes.

00:04:53.238 --> 00:04:56.447
It was ironic, 'cause I was
not only thinking of clothes,

00:04:56.447 --> 00:04:57.447
I was really

00:04:58.883 --> 00:05:01.693
going out with girls, I
was trying to look good,

00:05:01.693 --> 00:05:03.276
see what was sharp.

00:05:06.436 --> 00:05:08.664
- Did you finally get that pair of shoes?

00:05:08.664 --> 00:05:11.062
- I don't think so,

00:05:11.062 --> 00:05:12.235
but I made them.

00:05:12.235 --> 00:05:13.764
What happened was later,

00:05:13.764 --> 00:05:16.367
I think they were inspirations.

00:05:16.367 --> 00:05:18.841
I think when you're
younger you have visions

00:05:18.841 --> 00:05:23.008
of what your life could
be and I said earlier that

00:05:24.204 --> 00:05:27.755
movies and books open the world for you

00:05:27.755 --> 00:05:30.761
and so maybe those shoes
represented something to me,

00:05:30.761 --> 00:05:32.412
I don't know at the time.

00:05:32.412 --> 00:05:34.641
They just looked great.

00:05:34.641 --> 00:05:38.314
Years later, I think I loved shoes and

00:05:38.314 --> 00:05:41.814
I've been able to, I think in some levels,

00:05:42.668 --> 00:05:43.585
growing up,

00:05:45.378 --> 00:05:47.896
was the platform of things that I wanted

00:05:47.896 --> 00:05:49.146
as I got older.

00:05:50.449 --> 00:05:53.449
Instead of looking at a store, I'd say

00:05:53.449 --> 00:05:56.373
I want to make those shoes,
but I can make them better.

00:05:56.373 --> 00:05:59.053
On some levels, I had
a very good childhood,

00:05:59.053 --> 00:06:02.357
and I had the clothes, at
some levels, hand me downs.

00:06:02.357 --> 00:06:05.274
But as I grew up, I was inspired to

00:06:06.643 --> 00:06:09.082
see if I could do things that were better.

00:06:09.082 --> 00:06:11.272
I grew up in America,
I had the same dreams

00:06:11.272 --> 00:06:13.077
that everyone else had.

00:06:13.077 --> 00:06:14.801
Started where I started.

00:06:14.801 --> 00:06:16.992
My dream was to have a
nice house for my family,

00:06:16.992 --> 00:06:18.601
to have a nice car,

00:06:18.601 --> 00:06:20.743
to have my kids go to good schools,

00:06:20.743 --> 00:06:23.085
to do all the things that everyone wants.

00:06:23.085 --> 00:06:26.047
And as I got there, I was able to do that.

00:06:26.047 --> 00:06:29.219
And as I worked harder,
I was able to improve it.

00:06:29.219 --> 00:06:32.815
There was no miracle, there
was no snap of the fingers.

00:06:32.815 --> 00:06:34.685
It was confidence,

00:06:34.685 --> 00:06:37.696
and I think confidence builds as you go on

00:06:37.696 --> 00:06:39.308
if you have a good success.

00:06:39.308 --> 00:06:43.069
Closing the door on
Bloomingdale's was at one point

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:45.986
sort of crazy, and at the same time

00:06:47.209 --> 00:06:49.126
was amazingly powerful.

00:06:50.097 --> 00:06:52.041
- Fashion is changing so fast,

00:06:52.041 --> 00:06:55.102
and competition is coming up every day

00:06:55.102 --> 00:06:57.327
with all these young designers.

00:06:57.327 --> 00:07:01.056
What has kept you going forward like this?

00:07:01.056 --> 00:07:02.639
- You just somehow,

00:07:03.494 --> 00:07:05.994
I've always admired longevity.

00:07:07.011 --> 00:07:09.363
I've admired people that

00:07:09.363 --> 00:07:11.506
were not of the only moment.

00:07:11.506 --> 00:07:13.839
People that can carry it on.

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:17.861
They're actors, there are musicians,

00:07:17.861 --> 00:07:20.278
there are people that came up

00:07:21.498 --> 00:07:22.831
in rock and roll

00:07:24.217 --> 00:07:26.030
and the great ones stayed.

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:27.780
Frank Sinatra lasted,

00:07:28.717 --> 00:07:31.134
the whole pile of names that,

00:07:31.975 --> 00:07:34.206
Paul McCartney and The Beatles,

00:07:34.206 --> 00:07:36.308
they didn't last for
five years or 10 years,

00:07:36.308 --> 00:07:38.417
they lasted for 40 years.

00:07:38.417 --> 00:07:41.271
I admired longevity and
I admired consistency,

00:07:41.271 --> 00:07:45.353
and I admired the individuality
of holding your own,

00:07:45.353 --> 00:07:48.158
standing and fighting
for what you believe in.

00:07:48.158 --> 00:07:50.467
And I think if there's anything
that I really believe in

00:07:50.467 --> 00:07:53.431
is that, and what made me keep going,

00:07:53.431 --> 00:07:55.451
I didn't get great reviews every year.

00:07:55.451 --> 00:07:57.844
People didn't just say
you're wonderful, in fact,

00:07:57.844 --> 00:08:00.364
they couldn't care less, Ralph Lauren.

00:08:00.364 --> 00:08:02.714
But the consumer loved Ralph Lauren.

00:08:02.714 --> 00:08:04.619
It's not the fashion editor.

00:08:04.619 --> 00:08:06.359
- [Oprah] Where are you most yourself,

00:08:06.359 --> 00:08:09.431
and when are you most yourself?

00:08:09.431 --> 00:08:10.482
- I'm always myself.

00:08:10.482 --> 00:08:11.685
- That's a great answer.

00:08:11.685 --> 00:08:13.844
But sitting here on the porch right now,

00:08:13.844 --> 00:08:16.154
in your dude stuff here?

00:08:16.154 --> 00:08:17.144
- It's a part of me.

00:08:17.144 --> 00:08:17.977
- It's a part of you.

00:08:17.977 --> 00:08:19.446
- I'm not a cowboy all the time,

00:08:19.446 --> 00:08:22.679
I don't propose to be a cowboy.

00:08:22.679 --> 00:08:24.230
But I love the west.

00:08:24.230 --> 00:08:27.036
I love ranching, I love the idea of it.

00:08:27.036 --> 00:08:28.483
- [Oprah] Is it like creating

00:08:28.483 --> 00:08:30.130
- Absolutely.
- little movies for yourself?

00:08:30.130 --> 00:08:32.727
- The way I work is i feel
like I'm doing movies.

00:08:32.727 --> 00:08:33.717
Everything I do.

00:08:33.717 --> 00:08:36.693
If you said just design
that dress for that girl

00:08:36.693 --> 00:08:38.552
or design, I couldn't do it.

00:08:38.552 --> 00:08:42.187
But if you say, she lives in a mansion,

00:08:42.187 --> 00:08:46.025
or she lives in SoHo and she's an artist,

00:08:46.025 --> 00:08:48.454
I could design exactly
how she should look,

00:08:48.454 --> 00:08:51.024
how her hair should be,
what she should be wearing,

00:08:51.024 --> 00:08:53.590
and I'd create a world around her.

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:55.115
And I'd pin her up on
the wall, and I said,

00:08:55.115 --> 00:08:56.837
now I know what she needs.

00:08:56.837 --> 00:08:58.894
- What are some of the
most challenging moments

00:08:58.894 --> 00:09:00.301
in your career?

00:09:00.301 --> 00:09:04.552
- Well, when I started out
and made the ties and shirts,

00:09:04.552 --> 00:09:07.783
I started to become successful.

00:09:07.783 --> 00:09:10.283
After about 10 years, I hired.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:15.053
I had started my company
by borrowing $50,000

00:09:15.053 --> 00:09:17.900
from another person in the industry.

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:20.222
He lent me the 50,000, became my partner.

00:09:20.222 --> 00:09:21.055
And so,

00:09:22.543 --> 00:09:24.275
all of a sudden we were growing

00:09:24.275 --> 00:09:27.946
and all of a sudden I got
a phone call from a banker

00:09:27.946 --> 00:09:32.113
and said, Mr. Lauren, your bills
are not being paid on time.

00:09:34.076 --> 00:09:38.526
I had a team thinking they were
doing all the right things.

00:09:38.526 --> 00:09:41.003
And all of a sudden, I found that I was

00:09:41.003 --> 00:09:42.824
in trouble financially because I was

00:09:42.824 --> 00:09:46.496
taking on too many things
and no one told me to stop.

00:09:46.496 --> 00:09:49.413
I was being applauded for many ways

00:09:50.632 --> 00:09:53.071
but on some levels, on a financial level,

00:09:53.071 --> 00:09:56.252
I was not being applauded, and

00:09:56.252 --> 00:09:58.648
one of the bankers came to me and said,

00:09:58.648 --> 00:10:01.037
Ralph, you're almost out of business.

00:10:01.037 --> 00:10:03.144
What are you going to do?

00:10:03.144 --> 00:10:05.582
- That was 10 years after you started?

00:10:05.582 --> 00:10:07.150
- That was about 10 years after I started.

00:10:07.150 --> 00:10:09.150
But I remember the pain.

00:10:12.625 --> 00:10:15.953
I took everything I had
saved, which was $100,000,

00:10:15.953 --> 00:10:18.396
and I put it back in the company.

00:10:18.396 --> 00:10:20.729
$100,000 was all I had left.

00:10:23.086 --> 00:10:25.254
And I decided to license

00:10:25.254 --> 00:10:29.477
some of the brands and have
some money coming in, and so

00:10:29.477 --> 00:10:32.941
it gave me a shot to go
and hire better people

00:10:32.941 --> 00:10:35.786
and so that was a very important moment

00:10:35.786 --> 00:10:39.703
of seeing success in
terms of artistic success,

00:10:40.862 --> 00:10:43.047
but failure in terms of

00:10:43.047 --> 00:10:46.964
a company that could go
wrong in its finances.

00:10:46.964 --> 00:10:49.864
And that was a very important time.

00:10:49.864 --> 00:10:53.781
And so that was a very
major moment in my life.

00:10:55.180 --> 00:10:59.180
I've had those every
five years, I had a show

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:01.288
I remember, doing a fashion show

00:11:01.288 --> 00:11:03.608
and the clothes weren't ready and

00:11:03.608 --> 00:11:06.906
the tailors were ironing
or sewing the clothes

00:11:06.906 --> 00:11:09.133
as the girl is about
to walk out the runway.

00:11:09.133 --> 00:11:10.695
- What did you figure out?

00:11:10.695 --> 00:11:13.264
What happened to make it so late?

00:11:13.264 --> 00:11:14.666
Too many things at hand?

00:11:14.666 --> 00:11:16.939
- A lack of not getting
the right professionals,

00:11:16.939 --> 00:11:19.039
not planning well,

00:11:19.039 --> 00:11:21.925
not thinking you've got it
going and all of a sudden.

00:11:21.925 --> 00:11:23.452
I said to myself as I was

00:11:23.452 --> 00:11:27.994
walking on Broadway, not
knowing where I was going,

00:11:27.994 --> 00:11:30.886
I said this is never
going to happen again.

00:11:30.886 --> 00:11:32.903
- [Oprah] Did you always
want to be a cowboy?

00:11:32.903 --> 00:11:34.551
- I wanted to be everything.

00:11:34.551 --> 00:11:35.787
- You wanted to be everything.

00:11:35.787 --> 00:11:37.524
- All at once, I wanted
to be a baseball player,

00:11:37.524 --> 00:11:38.926
I wanted to be a basketball player,

00:11:38.926 --> 00:11:40.492
and I wanted to be an actor,
I wanted to be a dancer.

00:11:40.492 --> 00:11:43.075
I think my whole life has been,

00:11:45.408 --> 00:11:47.638
actually, I wanted to be Batman.

00:11:47.638 --> 00:11:50.493
That's a secret I'm telling you (laughing)

00:11:50.493 --> 00:11:52.405
and everyone out there.

00:11:52.405 --> 00:11:55.401
But the ability to stay young,

00:11:55.401 --> 00:11:57.801
the ability to enjoy

00:11:57.801 --> 00:11:59.745
what you enjoyed when you were a kid.

00:11:59.745 --> 00:12:02.094
Not to give up the hope and the excitement

00:12:02.094 --> 00:12:03.368
is very important.

00:12:03.368 --> 00:12:05.137
I remember meeting a friend of mine,

00:12:05.137 --> 00:12:06.624
her name was Nina Hyde,

00:12:06.624 --> 00:12:09.558
and she then told me that
she had breast cancer.

00:12:09.558 --> 00:12:13.475
(sentimental orchestral music)

00:12:14.839 --> 00:12:16.487
I went to the fashion industry and said

00:12:16.487 --> 00:12:17.639
let's go after this

00:12:17.639 --> 00:12:20.806
and give it some exposure and support.

00:12:22.847 --> 00:12:24.289
I got very caught up in it

00:12:24.289 --> 00:12:26.925
and then she passed away.

00:12:26.925 --> 00:12:28.700
And then one day I said you know,

00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:30.311
what else can I do?

00:12:30.311 --> 00:12:34.228
(sentimental orchestral music)

00:12:39.821 --> 00:12:42.751
I worked on breast cancer,
I certainly didn't solve it.

00:12:42.751 --> 00:12:44.728
But maybe there's more I can do,

00:12:44.728 --> 00:12:47.861
and I went to Sloan Kettering.

00:12:47.861 --> 00:12:50.006
- I was called to
Memorial Sloan Kettering,

00:12:50.006 --> 00:12:51.613
and it was in that meeting

00:12:51.613 --> 00:12:53.547
that I had the opportunity to tell

00:12:53.547 --> 00:12:56.434
Mr. Ralph Lauren what I saw as a problem

00:12:56.434 --> 00:12:59.816
and what I thought could
be a part of a solution.

00:12:59.816 --> 00:13:01.302
He stood up and shook my hand

00:13:01.302 --> 00:13:04.934
and he said, Dr. Freeman, I will help you.

00:13:04.934 --> 00:13:07.407
And that is how the Ralph
Lauren Center began.

00:13:07.407 --> 00:13:12.157
(sentimental orchestral music)

00:13:12.157 --> 00:13:15.423
- He talked about people
in under-served areas

00:13:15.423 --> 00:13:18.560
are dying because they can't get help.

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:20.830
I felt that sensitivity.

00:13:20.830 --> 00:13:22.646
Helping somebody who
doesn't have the funds,

00:13:22.646 --> 00:13:24.087
who doesn't have the know-how.

00:13:24.087 --> 00:13:26.991
Cancer is not only for the
poor and it's not for the rich.

00:13:26.991 --> 00:13:28.991
It happens to everybody.

00:13:31.134 --> 00:13:33.033
- We really try to educate people about

00:13:33.033 --> 00:13:34.642
what are the things that they're doing

00:13:34.642 --> 00:13:38.707
that are putting them at risk for cancer.

00:13:38.707 --> 00:13:39.784
- I'm really grateful.

00:13:39.784 --> 00:13:42.221
The chemotherapy process
itself was kind of grueling,

00:13:42.221 --> 00:13:43.998
but they really helped me a lot here.

00:13:43.998 --> 00:13:45.109
Everyone that you deal with

00:13:45.109 --> 00:13:48.248
has just this warm personality that

00:13:48.248 --> 00:13:50.553
is very inviting and very comforting

00:13:50.553 --> 00:13:52.745
considering the situation that you're in.

00:13:52.745 --> 00:13:56.662
(sentimental orchestral music)

00:13:58.567 --> 00:14:01.211
- 1,000 people are here today.

00:14:01.211 --> 00:14:03.305
(crowd cheerinG)

00:14:03.305 --> 00:14:05.244
And in England, and in Milan

00:14:05.244 --> 00:14:07.758
we have a walk and a march

00:14:07.758 --> 00:14:09.591
for a very good cause.

00:14:10.478 --> 00:14:12.092
And I want to thank you.

00:14:12.092 --> 00:14:16.009
(sentimental orchestral music)

00:14:29.439 --> 00:14:31.262
- We're here tonight because

00:14:31.262 --> 00:14:33.942
Ralph Lauren is a life saver.

00:14:33.942 --> 00:14:36.391
Long before everybody was
wearing the pink ribbons,

00:14:36.391 --> 00:14:39.357
Ralph Lauren was galvanizing
the fashion industry's

00:14:39.357 --> 00:14:41.524
response to breast cancer.

00:14:42.819 --> 00:14:46.736
(sentimental orchestral music)

00:14:50.051 --> 00:14:51.898
- The generous in The Royal Marsden

00:14:51.898 --> 00:14:56.406
by Ralph Lauren, a dedicated
supporter of cancer research,

00:14:56.406 --> 00:14:58.970
incredibly generously, they will fund

00:14:58.970 --> 00:15:03.526
The Ralph Lauren Center
For Breast Cancer Research.

00:15:03.526 --> 00:15:06.336
A world class research
facility at the Royal Marsden's

00:15:06.336 --> 00:15:07.753
Chelsea Hospital.

00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:11.043
- Cancer is scary.

00:15:11.043 --> 00:15:13.311
It's scary on every level and

00:15:13.311 --> 00:15:15.455
whatever I can do in this world

00:15:15.455 --> 00:15:18.288
to help a little bit, I try to do.

00:15:19.376 --> 00:15:21.405
It used to be cancer was a whisper,

00:15:21.405 --> 00:15:23.795
or breast cancer was a whisper.

00:15:23.795 --> 00:15:27.346
Women didn't even talk about
it, didn't know about it.

00:15:27.346 --> 00:15:29.991
Today it's not that way.

00:15:29.991 --> 00:15:31.889
And I think that's progress.

00:15:31.889 --> 00:15:33.670
Are we talking about
research into the future,

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:37.538
this is an ongoing commitment
that you stay with.

00:15:37.538 --> 00:15:40.591
- The Ralph Lauren Breast
Cancer Research Center

00:15:40.591 --> 00:15:42.408
at The Royal Marsden will bring together

00:15:42.408 --> 00:15:44.262
world renowned clinicians and scientists

00:15:44.262 --> 00:15:48.429
to really take forward our
research into better diagnosis

00:15:49.339 --> 00:15:51.479
and treatment of breast cancer.

00:15:51.479 --> 00:15:53.298
- I'm enormously grateful to Ralph Lauren

00:15:53.298 --> 00:15:55.007
for his generosity.

00:15:55.007 --> 00:15:57.741
You should know that
what you do saves lives

00:15:57.741 --> 00:16:01.008
and gives hope to thousands of people.

00:16:01.008 --> 00:16:04.925
(sentimental orchestral music)

00:16:23.140 --> 00:16:24.335
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:16:24.335 --> 00:16:26.691
I made this video because Abigail Rosser,

00:16:26.691 --> 00:16:29.129
I hope I pronounced that
properly, asked me to.

00:16:29.129 --> 00:16:31.106
If there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want me

00:16:31.106 --> 00:16:33.414
to profile next, leave
it in the comments below

00:16:33.414 --> 00:16:34.698
and I'll see what I can do.

00:16:34.698 --> 00:16:36.389
I'd also love to know
which of Ralph Lauren's

00:16:36.389 --> 00:16:38.872
top 10 rules had the
biggest impact on you.

00:16:38.872 --> 00:16:41.102
Leave it in the comments and
I'll join the discussion.

00:16:41.102 --> 00:16:42.670
Thank you so much for watching,

00:16:42.670 --> 00:16:46.170
continue to believe and I'll see you soon.

